{"runId":"20260919T094232-reasoning","condition":"reasoning","questionId":"q50","difficulty":10,"category":"logic","slot":"A","model":"glm-5.3-flash","expected":"3","at":"2026-09-19T09:44:52.929Z","raw":"**3 weighings.**\n\nWith 3 weighings, the balance scale has 3 possible outcomes each time, giving 3³ = 27 distinguishable outcome sequences. The problem has 24 possibilities (12 coins × heavy or light), so 3 weighings suffice in information-theoretic terms — and a concrete strategy exists:\n\n1. **Weighing 1:** Put 4 coins vs. 4 coins (say ABCD vs. EFGH), leaving IJKL aside.\n   - **Balanced:** The fake is among IJKL. Weigh I, J vs. K, A (known genuine). If balanced, L is fake — weigh L vs. A to determine heavy/light. If unbalanced, the direction tells you whether the suspect is heavy or light, and one more weighing (e.g., I vs. J) pins down which coin.\n   - **Unbalanced:** Say ABCD is heavier. Then either one of ABCD is heavy or one of EFGH is light. Weigh ABE vs. CFI (swapping some coins, using known-genuine I).\n     - If ABE heavier: A, B, or E is the culprit (A or B heavy, or E light) — weigh A vs. B: if balanced, E is light; otherwise the heavier of A, B is fake.\n     - If ABE lighter: C is heavy or F is light — weigh C vs. a genuine coin.\n     - If balanced: D is heavy or G/H is light — weigh G vs. H: if balanced, D is heavy; otherwise the lighter one is fake.\n\nSince 2 weighings give only 3² = 9 outcomes < 24 possibilities, 2 weighings cannot guarantee a solution.\n\nANSWER: 3","extracted":"3","correct":true,"latencyMs":29553,"promptTokens":105,"completionTokens":391,"reasoningTokens":20,"retried":false}
